# SINH(3M)

## NAME

sinh, cosh, tanh - hyperbolic functions

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <math.h>

double sinh(double x);

double cosh(double x);

double tanh(double x);
```

## DESCRIPTION

These functions compute the designated hyperbolic functions for real arguments.

## SEE ALSO

exp(3), sin(3), intro(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Sinh* and *cosh* return HUGE_VAL of appropriate sign when the correct value would overflow,
and set *errno* to ERANGE. **Note:** v7's page did not mention *errno*. The value comes from
exp(3), which is where the gate is; see BESM-6 NOTES.

*Tanh* is bounded by 1 and has no error return.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The thresholds were mantissa widths, and had to be rederived

For a large argument the exp(-x) term stops contributing and all three routines drop it. v7
puts that point at **21**, which is where it dies against a *56-bit* PDP-11 mantissa. The
mantissa here is 40 bits, so the term dies at 20*ln2 = 13.86: past |x| = 14, exp(-2x) is
already below 2\*\*-40, and exp(x) - exp(-x) and exp(x) agree to the last bit. **All three
routines switch at 14.**

### The large branch is spelled exp(x - ln2), not exp(x)/2

Identical arithmetic, and the difference is which routine raises the error.

Exp(3) gates its own argument at 43.6 = ln(DBL_MAX), so **exp(x)/2** would come back as a
saturated HUGE_VAL/2 for *x* in (43.6, 44.3] - where the true *sinh*, being half of exp(x), is
still perfectly representable. Writing it **exp(x - ln2)** moves *exp*'s gate out to x = 44.3 =
ln(2*DBL_MAX), which is exactly where *sinh* itself overflows, and lets *exp* raise the ERANGE
at the right place.

This matters more here than it would elsewhere, because on this machine an overflow past 2**63
is an arithmetic fault that **kills the program,** not an infinity to return. A gate one tenth
too loose does not produce a wrong number; it produces no number at all.

